  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "katya",
   "t": "Спасибо, я не голодна.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Thank you, I am not hungry.' In Russian, the idea of 'thank you' flows naturally into 'hungry' without extra filler words.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "katya",
   "t": "Я после шести не ем.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'I do not eat after six.' The grammar packs 'I' and 'to eat' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "denis",
   "t": "Кать, ну прости.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Katya, please forgive me.' Notice how Russian expresses 'Katya' and 'forgive' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "denis",
   "t": "У меня, правда, работы полно.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'I really do have tons of work.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'at' before landing on 'plenty'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "katya",
   "t": "Ладно, я тебя жду.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Alright, I am waiting for you.' The meaning hinges on 'all right' working together with 'am waiting for' in spoken Russian.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "katya",
   "t": "Приходи скорее в номер.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Come to the suite quickly.' Katya anchors the line on 'to come', while 'number' completes the thought.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "katya",
   "t": "Поцелуй меня.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Kiss me.' The grammar packs 'kiss' and 'me' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "denis",
   "t": "Че творишь, а?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'What are you doing, huh?' In Russian, the idea of 'what' flows naturally into 'and' without extra filler words.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "denis",
   "t": "Меня же шеф убьет.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'The Chef will kill me.' In Russian, the idea of 'me' flows naturally into 'will kill' without extra filler words.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "denis",
   "t": "Спокойно, Сири, говорю.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Calm down, I am talking to Siri.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'calmly' and finishing on 'speak'.",
